 Flat-buffer variant: rdi = buffer address, rsi = offset, rdx = length.
 Probes port 1297 (0x511) for the codex-vm bulk blit; on the magic
 answer 183 (0xB7) the guest stores address and length into the fixed
 cells 36160 and 36168 and rings doorbell 1296 (0x510) with command 3,
 so the whole buffer leaves in one VM exit. Anywhere else (older VM,
 QEMU, real hardware) the port floats 255 and the UART loop below runs
 instead. emit-com1-init writes FCR = 0xC7, so the 16550 transmit FIFO
 is on and LSR bit 5 means the whole 16-byte FIFO is empty rather than
 one holding register: poll it once, then rep outsb a burst of up to
 16. A rep string I/O access is a single trap under WHP and KVM, so a
 burst costs two exits where sixteen single bytes cost thirty-two.

