 EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL is Revision at 0, Media at 8, Reset at 0x10, ReadBlocks
 at 0x18, WriteBlocks at 0x20, FlushBlocks at 0x28. The flush is not optional
 here: the operator pulls the stick, and a write left in the firmware's cache
 is a write that did not happen.

 Which DEVICE the helper talks to is decided before any of that, and the two
 bindings above differ in what they promise. LocateProtocol (BootServices +
 0x140) returns the first handle in the firmware's handle database carrying
 the GUID, and the UEFI spec leaves that order unspecified: on a board with
 more than one disk, or with per-partition Block I/O handles, the first one
 is not necessarily the medium we booted from. EFI_LOADED_IMAGE_PROTOCOL
 (Revision at 0, ParentHandle at 8, SystemTable at 16, DeviceHandle at 24),
 reached by HandleProtocol (BootServices + 0x98) on the ImageHandle the
 firmware passed at entry, names in DeviceHandle exactly the device the
 image was loaded from. So the helpers try ImageHandle -> LoadedImage ->
 DeviceHandle -> Block I/O first, and fall back to LocateProtocol when the
 stub primed no handle or any link in that chain refuses. A DeviceHandle is
 normally a PARTITION handle, whose LBA 0 is the partition's own first
 sector rather than the disk's -- the mount side owns that difference.

 R10 is the Codex heap pointer and is volatile in the Microsoft x64 ABI, so the
 firmware may clobber it. It is saved in R15 and restored. RBX and R12 to R15
 are non-volatile there, which is why values survive across the calls.

  emit-uefi-block-write-sector-helper : CodegenState -> CodegenState

  emit-uefi-block-write-sector-helper (st) =

   let fn-off = st.code-len

   in let n = list-push (st.fo-names) "block-write-sector"

