 The 32-bit MMIO pair. Almost every peripheral register on the target
 MCUs is a 32-bit word, and many of them REQUIRE a single aligned
 word access -- assembling one out of four byte reads is not the same
 transaction and on real silicon does not work. read-mmio and
 poke-mmio above are byte-width, which is why the board HAL could not
 use them and shipped `mmio-read-32 (addr) = 0` instead.

 RDI is the base, RSI the offset, RDX the value. The load is
 `mov eax, [rdi]` (8B 07), which zero-extends into RAX by the x86-64
 rule that a 32-bit write clears the upper half; the store is
 `mov [rdi], edx` (89 17).

